# Random search

Random search draws independent points uniformly from a finite box and
retains the best point seen. Basin groups new evaluations into batches of
`lambda` candidates and carries the previous best into the next population.
It uses objective values alone, without learning directions or adapting a
local search scale.

[API reference](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/random_search/struct.RandomSearch.html)

| Property | Requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Objective | A scalar function |
| Evaluations | `CostFunction` |
| Search | Independent uniform sampling with an elite carried between batches |
| Constraints | A box-only declaration through `Constraints`; finite bounds |
| Parameters | All four dense backends, with `f32` or `f64` |

## When to use it

Use random search as a simple baseline for a bounded problem, to explore an
unfamiliar objective, or to generate candidate starting points. Independent
draws can reach separated basins without crossing the objective barriers
between them. The broader theory of random-search methods appears in
[Solis and Wets (1981)](#ref-solis-wets-1981).

The method uses every new evaluation to cover the same box. It does not
concentrate future draws around promising points, so precise optimization
can require many samples. Compare [differential evolution](../de/) or
[particle swarm optimization](../global-best-pso/) when population feedback
could make better use of the budget. For randomized local improvement from
one starting point, see [Solis–Wets](../../derivative-free/solis-wets/).

Uniform sampling becomes less effective when good solutions occupy a small
fraction of a high-dimensional box. If a useful region occupies fraction
`p` of the box, `N` independent draws hit it with probability
`1 - (1 - p)^N`. This describes coverage, rather than the probability of
finding an exact continuous minimizer.

## How it works

Initialization draws `lambda` members uniformly inside the problem's box,
evaluates them, and sorts them by objective value. You can instead supply
an explicit population with exactly that many members.

Each subsequent iteration saves the best member and its known cost, then
draws and evaluates `lambda` new independent candidates. It sorts those
new candidates together with the saved elite and retains the best `lambda`
members. The retained elite is not reevaluated. With finite deterministic
costs, the reported best objective therefore never increases.

Only that one elite affects the next iteration. The remaining population
members do not contribute directions, mutation scales, or a sampling
distribution. Changing `lambda` mainly changes batch size, observation
frequency, and the number of evaluations per iteration.

After `k` complete iterations, the solver has used
`lambda * (k + 1)` objective evaluations, including initialization. With
`lambda = 1`, it retains one incumbent and compares one fresh draw against
it per iteration.

## Example

Search a two-dimensional quadratic over `[-5, 5]^2`. Its minimum is zero
at `[1.0, -2.0]`. Random search can find a useful approximation within this
budget, but the example deliberately checks a modest objective threshold
instead of expecting the accuracy of a local solver.

<!-- compile: solver-random-search -->
```rust
use basin::{
    BoxBounds, ConstraintDescription, ConstraintSet, Constraints,
    CostFunction, Executor, PopulationProgress, RandomSearch,
};
use std::convert::Infallible;

struct BoundedQuadratic {
    lower: Vec<f64>,
    upper: Vec<f64>,
}

impl CostFunction for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Param = Vec<f64>;
    type Output = f64;
    type Error = Infallible;

    fn cost(&self, x: &Self::Param) -> Result<f64, Self::Error> {
        Ok((x[0] - 1.0).powi(2) + (x[1] + 2.0).powi(2))
    }
}

impl Constraints for BoundedQuadratic {
    type Set = ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<Vec<f64>>>;

    fn constraints(&self) -> <Self::Set as ConstraintDescription>::Borrowed<'_> {
        ConstraintSet::new().with_bounds(BoxBounds::new(
            Some(&self.lower),
            Some(&self.upper),
        ))
    }
}

fn main() {
    let problem = BoundedQuadratic {
        lower: vec![-5.0; 2],
        upper: vec![5.0; 2],
    };
    let solver = RandomSearch::new(32, 42);

    let result = Executor::new(
        problem,
        solver,
        PopulationProgress::<Vec<f64>>::empty(),
    )
    .max_iter(1_000)
    .run()
    .unwrap();

    assert!(result.cost() < 0.05);
    assert!((-5.0..=5.0).contains(&result.param()[0]));
    assert!((-5.0..=5.0).contains(&result.param()[1]));
}
```

The fixed seed makes the example reproducible. Reaching the iteration
limit reports that the requested search budget was used; it does not
certify that the best point is globally optimal.

## Practical considerations

### Choose meaningful bounds

The box defines the sampling distribution. Very wide bounds spend most
evaluations far from a useful region, while bounds that exclude a good
solution prevent the solver from finding it. If a parameter varies over
orders of magnitude, consider optimizing its logarithm: a uniform draw in
the transformed parameter corresponds to a different distribution in the
original units.

Declare `ConstraintSet<BoxBounds<V>>` with both bound vectors present.
They must be non-empty, finite, equally sized, and ordered. Equal bounds
are allowed and fix that coordinate. The solver rejects additional
constraint blocks; it samples the box rather than a general feasible set.

### Budget objective calls, not just iterations

`RandomSearch::new(lambda, seed)` requires a positive batch size. A larger
batch uses more cost evaluations per iteration and can expose more
independent work to the `parallel` feature. Compare runs using cost
evaluations when their batch sizes differ.

Executor budgets are checked between complete iterations. Initialization
evaluates a full batch, and the next iteration can finish a batch that
crosses `max_cost_evals`. Choose `lambda` with that granularity in mind
when objective calls are expensive.

### Use patience when checking progress

There is no default solver convergence criterion. Set execution limits
such as `max_iter`, `max_cost_evals`, or `max_time`, and use a target
objective if a useful threshold is known.

Optional step- and cost-change checks compare successive elite points.
An iteration without a new record has zero change, even though the next
independent batch could still improve the result. A stagnation window is
often more appropriate than stopping after one unchanged representative.
For a noisy objective, retaining the smallest observed cost can preserve
an unusually favorable observation, so validate the returned point with
additional evaluations outside the search.

## Implementation

`PopulationProgress` stores the population and its matching objective
values, with the best member as its current representative. `RandomSearch`
owns a `ChaCha8Rng` and the configured seed. It samples proposals in a
fixed order and batches independent objective evaluations; the `parallel`
feature preserves that order for a deterministic objective.

`PopulationProgress::from_population` accepts exactly `lambda` finite
vectors with the bounds' dimension. Fresh initialization resets progress
and the RNG, projects those members into the box, and reevaluates them.
This is a population warm start. Since future draws cover the entire box,
the supplied members do not create a local sampling region.

An empty state with the same seed repeats the original run. An exact
checkpoint must retain solver, state, and evaluation counts together so
that it preserves the live RNG and skips initialization. Both solver and
progress support serialization with the `serde` feature; state alone
cannot resume the random stream exactly.

Supported parameter types are `Vec<F>`, `nalgebra::DVector<F>`,
`ndarray::Array1<F>`, and `faer::Col<F>`, with `F = f32` or `f64`. Random
search requires uniform box sampling and coordinate access, without matrix
operations or a BLAS/LAPACK provider. See the API's [Backends
section](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/random_search/struct.RandomSearch.html#backends)
for custom-vector requirements.

